# Cloudflare Pages header rules.
#
# Why this exists: on 2026-08-08 a deploy shipped an index.html referencing
# app.js?v=50 while the edge still held the PREVIOUS app.js. Browsers stored
# that stale body under the new ?v=50 url and, because assets were served with
# max-age=14400, kept serving it for four hours. Version-bumping — the entire
# cache-busting strategy this app relies on — silently failed, and the app ran
# code that did not match its own HTML.
#
# no-cache does not mean "don't store": it means "revalidate before use". An
# unchanged file still costs only a 304, so this is cheap, and a stale body can
# never survive a reload again.
#
# NOTE: a zone-level "Browser Cache TTL" set in the Cloudflare dashboard
# overrides these headers. It must be set to "Respect Existing Headers" for
# this file to take effect.
/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache
